# PaleONT — T-Box

@prefix cito: <http://purl.org/spar/cito/> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@prefix foaf: <http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/> .
@prefix owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix po: <https://w3id.org/paleont/> .
@prefix rdf: <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

cito:agreesWith rdfs:subPropertyOf po:engagesWith .

cito:disagreesWith rdfs:subPropertyOf po:engagesWith .

cito:updates rdfs:subPropertyOf po:engagesWith .

po: a owl:Ontology ;
    dcterms:description """Models the epistemic structure of palaeopathological
        reporting: observations, the claims grounded in them, the frameworks
        under which those claims are made, and the adjudication between
        competing claims."""@en ;
    dcterms:title "PaleONT — an ontology of palaeopathological claims"@en ;
    owl:versionInfo "0.1.0" .

po:aboutTechnique a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "about technique"@en ;
    rdfs:domain po:MethodologicalClaim ;
    rdfs:range po:Technique .

po:appliedCorrection a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "applied correction"@en ;
    rdfs:domain po:DatingClaim ;
    rdfs:range po:Correction .

po:appliedTo a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "applied to"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Decision D5. A methodological claim may NOT have
        po:aboutSpecimen — the restriction above forbids it, because its
        subject matter is a technique, not a skeleton. But a judgement about a
        method is often reached in the course of examining particular
        specimens, and the source says so: "the results of the archaeometric
        analyses APPLIED HERE cannot elucidate the origin".

        po:appliedTo records that occasion without claiming the judgement is
        about the skeleton. "CT cannot separate the treponematoses" remains a
        statement about CT; po:appliedTo says where that was found out.

        Same shape of problem as D4, same shape of solution: when one property
        is being asked to mean two things, split it rather than equivocate or
        forbid."""@en ;
    rdfs:domain po:MethodologicalClaim ;
    rdfs:range po:Specimen .

po:confidenceInterval a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "confidence interval"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """The statistical confidence at which the bounds are stated:
        "1 sigma", "95% CI", or "archaeological" for an interval that carries
        no statistical claim at all.

        Decision D7. Without this, CQ4b compares two intervals as if their
        endpoints were hard boundaries — which is the very reduction this
        project argues against, committed by our own query. A calibrated
        radiocarbon interval is not a fact with edges: it is a hedged
        estimate, and the source says so ("the precision of the radiocarbon
        dates of medieval samples are often better than ±25 to 30 years for
        the 1 sigma statistical uncertainty").

        Consequence: a disagreement computed from bare bounds is a claim about
        the bounds, not about the individual. queries/cq04b_tolerant.rq applies
        the source's OWN stated precision and reports a different — and, as it
        turns out, more faithful — result."""@en ;
    rdfs:domain po:CalibratedInterval ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string .

po:declaredIn a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "declared in"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """NO rdfs:domain, deliberately. Observations are published in
        a source just as claims are, and an observation is not a claim — the
        two classes are disjoint. Declaring the domain as po:Claim made every
        observation carrying this property inferred to be a Claim, and the
        ontology inconsistent. HermiT caught it. Either the domain goes, or a
        union class is introduced; the union buys nothing here, so it goes."""@en ;
    rdfs:range po:Source .

po:discriminatesBetween a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "discriminates between"@en ;
    rdfs:domain po:MethodologicalClaim ;
    rdfs:range po:Condition .

po:endYear a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "end year"@en ;
    rdfs:domain po:CalibratedInterval ;
    rdfs:range xsd:integer .

po:framesContent a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "frames content"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """The system of thought TO WHICH the content of the claim
        belongs. Distinct from po:underFramework, which records where the
        claimant stands.

        Decision D4. A modern study asserting that an individual was treated
        with an Hg-containing medicine makes a MODERN claim whose content
        belongs to the MEDIEVAL humoral frame: mercury counted as a medicine
        because medieval medicine said so. Collapsing the two into one
        property forced a choice between a false uniformity (everything
        modern, CQ8b empty) and an equivocation (the property meaning two
        different things depending on the claim).

        Deliberately NOT functional: a claim may frame content belonging to
        more than one system — the mercury case arguably frames both a modern
        chemical measurement and a medieval therapeutic act — and closing that
        off buys nothing.

        The consequence is sharper than the version it replaces: the two
        frames do NOT coexist as equals. One contains the other. The paper
        does not look at Sk. 90 from inside the medieval frame; it looks from
        outside and REPRESENTS that frame. There is no standpoint from which
        both can be seen externally."""@en ;
    rdfs:domain po:Claim ;
    rdfs:range po:DiagnosticFramework .

po:groundedIn a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "grounded in"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """THE boundary property. Everything on the left is evidence,
        everything on the right is interpretation. Not functional: one claim
        may rest on several observations. See decision D1 on whether the
        range should widen to include po:Claim."""@en ;
    rdfs:domain po:Claim ;
    rdfs:range po:Observation .

po:hasDiscriminatingPower a owl:FunctionalProperty,
        owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "has discriminating power"@en ;
    rdfs:domain po:MethodologicalClaim ;
    rdfs:range po:DiscriminatingPower .

po:hasEffect a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "has effect"@en ;
    rdfs:domain po:ErrorSource ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string .

po:hasErrorSource a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "has error source"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Domain is the technique: a known limitation holds always."@en ;
    rdfs:domain po:Technique ;
    rdfs:range po:ErrorSource .

po:hasResult a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "has result"@en ;
    rdfs:domain po:DatingClaim ;
    rdfs:range po:CalibratedInterval ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf po:proposes .

po:hedge a owl:FunctionalProperty,
        owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "hedge"@en ;
    rdfs:domain po:Claim ;
    rdfs:range po:ConfidenceLevel .

po:hedgeWording a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "hedge wording"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """The source's EXACT words — 'cannot be excluded',
        'consistent with'. po:hedge gives the normalised level for querying;
        this keeps the original phrasing, so that our normalisation never
        silently replaces the author's own hedging."""@en ;
    rdfs:domain po:Claim ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string .

po:heldBy a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "held by"@en ;
    rdfs:domain po:Claim ;
    rdfs:range foaf:Agent .

po:isNegated a owl:DatatypeProperty,
        owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:label "is negated"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Decision D6. The claim asserts the NEGATIVE of its content:
        not "leprosy is proposed" but "leprosy is denied", not "treated with
        mercury" but "not treated with mercury".

        WHY IT WAS NEEDED. Every property in this model was affirmative, so a
        source could be recorded only when it asserted something. Schwarz et
        al. say of the Refshale and Tirup individuals that they "were not
        exposed to high amounts of Hg during their life" — a claim, with an
        agent, a ground and a hedge, that the model simply could not hold.
        That asymmetry was itself a position: it made denial invisible while
        assertion was fully represented.

        PRECEDENT. The course slides use exactly this device in the Ferguson
        unit tests — "who is refused to receive what?" is answered with
        ?e :isNegated "true", and the affirmative queries carry
        FILTER NOT EXISTS { ?e :isNegated "true" }. Ours is typed boolean
        rather than a string, but the pattern is theirs.

        NOT THE SAME AS po:rejects. A RejectionClaim refuses SOMEONE ELSE'S
        CLAIM — layer 3, a claim about a claim. po:isNegated is layer 1: the
        claim's own content is asserted negatively. "I reject your diagnosis"
        and "I claim he was not treated" are different acts.

        NO TENSION WITH NCQ3. A negated claim is still a claim: it carries an
        agent, a framework and a hedge. The model still cannot say that an
        individual WAS healthy; it can say that someone denied a particular
        condition for them. Denials are perspectival too — which is the thesis
        applied symmetrically rather than an exception to it.

        ABSENCE MEANS AFFIRMATIVE. Claims without this property are positive.
        Consequence: every query that reports what a source asserts must carry
        FILTER NOT EXISTS { ?claim po:isNegated true }, or it will report a
        denial as an assertion. All affected queries were updated."""@en ;
    rdfs:domain po:Claim ;
    rdfs:range xsd:boolean .

po:maxAge a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "maximum age"@en ;
    rdfs:domain po:AgeClaim ;
    rdfs:range xsd:integer .

po:minAge a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "minimum age"@en ;
    rdfs:domain po:AgeClaim ;
    rdfs:range xsd:integer .

po:observedOn a owl:FunctionalProperty,
        owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "observed on"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Functional: one observation concerns one specimen."@en ;
    rdfs:domain po:Observation ;
    rdfs:range po:Specimen .

po:observesLesionType a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "observes lesion type"@en ;
    rdfs:domain po:Observation ;
    rdfs:range po:LesionType .

po:onBone a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "on bone"@en ;
    rdfs:domain po:Observation ;
    rdfs:range po:Bone .

po:proposesCondition a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "proposes condition"@en ;
    rdfs:domain po:DiagnosticClaim ;
    rdfs:range po:Condition ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf po:proposes .

po:proposesTreatment a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "proposes treatment"@en ;
    rdfs:domain po:TreatmentClaim ;
    rdfs:range po:Treatment ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf po:proposes .

po:rejects a owl:IrreflexiveProperty,
        owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "rejects"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """RANGE IS po:Claim, NOT po:Condition. This single line is
        the thesis expressed as an axiom: one rejects an assertion, not a
        disease. A rejection says nothing about whether the individual was
        ill."""@en ;
    rdfs:domain po:RejectionClaim ;
    rdfs:range po:Claim ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf po:engagesWith .

po:specimenID a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "specimen ID"@en ;
    rdfs:domain po:Specimen ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string .

po:startYear a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "start year"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """xsd:integer, NOT xsd:gYear. Two reasons. (1) xsd:gYear is
        not part of the OWL 2 datatype map, so HermiT refuses to load an
        ontology that uses it — the reasoner step fails before it starts.
        (2) integers compare arithmetically, which CQ4b needs, and negative
        values express BCE without special handling."""@en ;
    rdfs:domain po:CalibratedInterval ;
    rdfs:range xsd:integer .

po:underFramework a owl:FunctionalProperty,
        owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "under framework"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """The standpoint FROM WHICH the claim is made — the frame the
        claimant occupies. Functional: a claim is made from one position. Two
        positions on the same content are two claims.
        For a single modern source every claim is ModernBiomedical, and that
        uniformity is a finding, not a defect: the paper never leaves the
        modern frame. What varies is po:framesContent."""@en ;
    rdfs:domain po:Claim ;
    rdfs:range po:DiagnosticFramework .

po:usedTechnique a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "used technique"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Domain is Observation, not Claim. A dating claim therefore
        reaches its technique through groundedIn/usedTechnique. This enforces
        that every use of a technique is an observation — see decision D2."""@en ;
    rdfs:domain po:Observation ;
    rdfs:range po:Technique .

po:weakerThan a owl:ObjectProperty,
        owl:TransitiveProperty ;
    rdfs:label "weaker than"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Orders the confidence levels. This ordering is OURS, not
        the source's, and must be declared as such in the documentation."""@en ;
    rdfs:domain po:ConfidenceLevel ;
    rdfs:range po:ConfidenceLevel .

po:Discriminating a po:DiscriminatingPower ;
    rdfs:label "discriminating"@en .

po:Inconclusive a po:DiscriminatingPower ;
    rdfs:label "inconclusive"@en .

po:MedievalHumoral a po:DiagnosticFramework ;
    rdfs:label "medieval humoral framework"@en .

po:ModernBiomedical a po:DiagnosticFramework ;
    rdfs:label "modern biomedical framework"@en .

po:Suggestive a po:DiscriminatingPower ;
    rdfs:label "suggestive"@en .

po:Tentative a po:ConfidenceLevel ;
    rdfs:label "tentative"@en ;
    po:weakerThan po:Possible .

po:aboutSpecimen a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "about specimen"@en ;
    rdfs:domain po:Claim ;
    rdfs:range po:Specimen .

po:Bone a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Bone"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Skeletal element. Individuals carry skos:exactMatch links
        to UBERON rather than using UBERON classes directly as values, which
        avoids punning."""@en .

po:Certain a po:ConfidenceLevel ;
    rdfs:label "certain"@en .

po:Correction a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Correction"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """An adjustment applied to one particular dating. A property
        of THIS dating: it holds here. Recording it is what turns an apparent
        contradiction between two dates into two declared treatments of one
        measurement — and prevents CQ4b from reporting false positives."""@en .

po:DiagnosticClaim a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Diagnostic claim"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Proposes a condition for a specimen."@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf po:Claim .

po:LesionType a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Lesion type"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """The type of a skeletal lesion, shared across specimens.
        The concrete occurrence is the po:Observation. This split is what
        makes CQ10 (recurrence across specimens) answerable."""@en .

po:Possible a po:ConfidenceLevel ;
    rdfs:label "possible"@en ;
    po:weakerThan po:Probable .

po:Probable a po:ConfidenceLevel ;
    rdfs:label "probable"@en ;
    po:weakerThan po:Certain .

po:RejectionClaim a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Rejection claim"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Rejects another claim. Note the range of po:rejects: a
        rejection is an act against an ASSERTION, never a verdict on the
        truth of a disease. Acquittal is not innocence."""@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf po:Claim .

po:Source a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Source"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A publication in which claims are declared."@en .

po:Treatment a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Treatment"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A therapeutic intervention received in life."@en .

po:TreatmentClaim a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Treatment claim"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Proposes a treatment received in life. Typically made
        under a historical framework, and typically grounded in a chemical
        observation such as mercury."""@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf po:Claim .

po:AgeClaim a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Age claim"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Proposes an age-at-death range. Age is a claim, not a
        property of the specimen — deliberately the same shape as a
        diagnosis."""@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf po:Claim .

po:Condition a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Condition"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """A disease or pathological condition. Individuals carry
        skos:exactMatch links to MONDO. NOTE: there is no path from
        po:Specimen to this class."""@en .

po:DatingClaim a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Dating claim"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Proposes a calibrated interval for a specimen."@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf po:Claim .

po:ErrorSource a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Error source"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """A known limitation of a technique — e.g. the marine
        reservoir effect. A property of the TECHNIQUE: it holds always.
        Contrast po:Correction."""@en .

po:proposes a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "proposes"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Superproperty. Never asserted directly; always inferred."@en ;
    rdfs:domain po:Claim .

po:Technique a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Technique"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Any means by which an observation is produced: naked-eye
        examination, radiography, radiocarbon dating, atomic absorption
        spectrometry. ONE class, no hierarchy. Ranking techniques by
        reliability is what the sources ARGUE about, so it must live in
        po:MethodologicalClaim, never in the schema."""@en .

po:engagesWith a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "engages with"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Superproperty. Never asserted directly; always inferred."@en ;
    rdfs:domain po:Claim ;
    rdfs:range po:Claim .

po:CalibratedInterval a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Calibrated interval"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """A dated range with numeric bounds. A class rather than a
        literal because CQ4b must COMPUTE disagreement by comparing two
        intervals, which a label cannot support."""@en .

po:DiagnosticFramework a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Diagnostic framework"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """The standpoint from which a claim is made — modern
        biomedical, medieval humoral. Two individuals carry the entire
        perspectival argument: the same specimen seen through two
        incommensurable lenses."""@en .

po:DiscriminatingPower a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Discriminating power"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """How far a technique is said to separate competing
        hypotheses. A small controlled list, so that 'which techniques
        cannot discriminate' is a value filter rather than a second
        question."""@en .

po:MethodologicalClaim a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Methodological claim"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """A claim about what a technique can or cannot establish.
        The meta level. Note that it has NO po:aboutSpecimen: its subject
        matter is a technique, not a skeleton. That absence is how the model
        distinguishes 'this individual had leprosy' from 'this method cannot
        tell leprosy from syphilis'."""@en ;
    rdfs:subClassOf [ a owl:Restriction ;
            owl:maxCardinality "0"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
            owl:onProperty po:aboutSpecimen ],
        po:Claim .

po:Specimen a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Specimen"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """A set of skeletal remains attributed to one individual.
        The anchor of the graph. Deliberately poor in properties: almost
        nothing is said ABOUT a specimen, because almost everything one might
        want to say is in fact a claim someone made."""@en .

po:Observation a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Observation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """One act of examining a specimen with a technique, yielding
        one datum. Carries what was seen, on which bone, by which technique.
        This is the concrete occurrence: the lesion TYPE is shared across
        specimens, the observation is not."""@en .

po:ConfidenceLevel a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Confidence level"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """The hedge attached to a claim, as an individual rather
        than a string or a number. A number would invent precision the source
        never produced; a string cannot be counted, grouped or ordered."""@en .

po:Claim a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Claim"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """A proposition held by an agent, under a framework, with a
        stated degree of confidence, grounded in one or more observations.
        Nothing enters this graph as fact; everything enters as something
        someone asserted."""@en .

[] a owl:AllDifferent ;
    owl:distinctMembers ( po:Discriminating po:Suggestive po:Inconclusive ) .

[] a owl:AllDifferent ;
    owl:distinctMembers ( po:ModernBiomedical po:MedievalHumoral ) .

[] a owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
    owl:members ( po:DiagnosticClaim po:RejectionClaim po:DatingClaim po:AgeClaim po:TreatmentClaim po:MethodologicalClaim ) .

[] a owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
    owl:members ( po:Specimen po:Observation po:Claim po:CalibratedInterval po:Technique po:LesionType po:Bone po:Condition po:DiagnosticFramework po:ConfidenceLevel po:Source po:ErrorSource po:Correction po:Treatment po:DiscriminatingPower ) .

[] a owl:AllDifferent ;
    owl:distinctMembers ( po:Certain po:Probable po:Possible po:Tentative ) .

